`theorem_4_4_inverses` says the pair written in the proof of Theorem 4.4, with \(i_\infty\) the embedding of the inverse limit into its function space and \(j_\infty\) the projection back, satisfies \(j_\infty \circ i_\infty = \mathrm{id}\) and \(i_\infty \circ j_\infty = \mathrm{id}\). The two identities are the composite identities of the projection after the embedding and of the embedding after the projection.

`theorem_4_4_orderIso` says the same pair is an order isomorphism between the inverse limit \(D_\infty\) and the function space \([D_\infty \to D_\infty]\). The underlying equivalence is inverse because each composite is the identity on its side, and both maps are monotone. Scott's abstract records that the spaces are isomorphic. The homeomorphism stated as Theorem 4.4 is the source-topology statement.

`theorem_4_4` says that if the starting lattice \(D_0\) is a continuous lattice, then the inverse limit \(D_\infty\) of the recursively defined tower of function spaces is a continuous lattice, and \(D_\infty\) is homeomorphic to its function space \([D_\infty \to D_\infty]\). The inverse limit carries the subspace topology induced from the product, and the function space carries the pointwise topology of Definition 3.1. Every level of the tower is a continuous lattice by induction on the level: the starting level is the hypothesis, and a successor level is a function space of continuous lattices, hence a continuous lattice by Theorem 3.3. Proposition 4.1 then makes the inverse limit a continuous lattice, and the source-topology form of the Theorem 4.4 homeomorphism supplies the homeomorphism.
